Learning Outcomes
By the end of these notes you can:

name the three parts every ggplot2 figure is built from, and point to each in a line of code;
tell mapping an aesthetic apart from setting one, and fix the bug that comes from confusing them;
choose between geom_histogram(), geom_bar(), geom_boxplot() and geom_point() from the kind of variable you are drawing;
use fill, colour and faceting to split a figure by group, and say which comparison each one makes easy;
give a figure a title and axis labels a reader outside the team can follow;
say when base R is the right tool and when ggplot2 is.

Learning Outcomes
By the end of these notes you can:

lay out a playtest report in nine parts, and say what question each part answers;
explain why survey exports arrive wide, and what makes a table tidy;
reshape a whole survey with pivot_longer() and summarise or plot every item in one step;
categorise free-text comments into a few themes, and say what they can and cannot support;
write a finding as What → Pattern → Meaning → Action, and diagnose a finding that skips a step;
explain why a Quarto report cannot quote a stale number.

Learning Outcomes
By the end of these notes you can:

classify a variable as nominal, ordinal, discrete or continuous, and say which operations that allows;
recognise a tidy table and name the most common way data fails to be one;
say what a sample is, and why every number you compute describes the sample rather than your audience;
choose between mean and median, and read the gap between them as evidence of skew;
describe spread with the standard deviation, quartiles and IQR, and say why the range is rarely enough;
draw a histogram and a boxplot in base R and say what each shows that a summary hides.

Learning Outcomes
By the end of these notes you can:

say what an NA means in a playtest export, and check whether the pattern of missing values is itself a finding;
find exact duplicates and duplicates on the key, and remove them;
tell an impossible value from an unusual one, and treat each correctly;
repair inconsistent category labels and convert text to numbers without losing data silently;
keep a cleaning log that records every row dropped and what it did to n;
use select(), filter(), mutate(), arrange(), group_by() and summarise(), composed with the pipe;
explain what group_by() + summarise() does by writing the same answer with tapply().

Learning Outcomes
By the end of these notes you can:

explain why a second group of players would give a different result, and why that matters before you act on any difference;
report an observed difference with its confidence interval, and read the interval as a range of plausible sizes;
state what a p-value is, and what it is not;
tell statistical significance apart from practical significance, with a Tidebreak example of each;
choose between an independent-samples and a paired t-test from how the playtest was designed;
check the assumptions of a t-test at a practical level, including with a Q-Q plot;
work through visualise → describe → estimate difference → confidence interval → statistical test → interpret in that order.

By the end of these notes you can:

explain why an average on its own cannot describe a playtest;
read a standard deviation as the typical distance of a player from the mean;
describe a distribution by its centre, spread, skew and outliers;
say whether an outlier is an error, an extreme player or the finding itself;
read the mean, median, standard deviation and high percentiles together and say what each adds;
draw hist(), boxplot(), barplot() and plot() in base R and say what each one is for.

By the end of these notes you can:

explain why an average on its own cannot describe a playtest;
read a standard deviation as the typical distance of a player from the mean;
describe a distribution by its centre, spread, skew and outliers;
say whether an outlier is an error, an extreme player or the finding itself;
read the mean, median, standard deviation and high percentiles together and say what each adds;
draw hist(), boxplot(), barplot() and plot() in base R and say what each one is for.

Learning Outcomes
By the end of these notes you can:

name the three cases where a team’s judgement of its own game reliably fails;
distinguish telemetry, performance capture and asking people, and say what each can and cannot tell you;
rewrite a vague design question as a measure you could record for a single player;
write down, before collecting anything, the result that would change your mind;
load a playtest CSV into R and take a first look at it with read_csv(), glimpse() and summary().
